BY MERIT, and the merit is that two of this scenario's three clauses measure a
capability that does not exist yet. Declared here so it does not red every batch
forever (AUTOTEST.md, "A scenario that is SUPPOSED to fail must say so").

What is and is not implemented, as of the item-64 held-axis-reinforcement batch:

  d3 SOLO DEPARTURE (beachhead line x=10) - addressed. PoiOffensiveBotModuleInfo.
     FreePoolMinAdvanceUnits stops StageFreePool ordering a lone free-pool unit forward
     when no axis exists. Expect this clause to read 2 with the gate at 2 and 1 with it
     at 0; the gate-0 arm is the control and it is REQUIRED to fail.

  d1 ADVANCE INTERVAL - the INTERVAL is not addressed; the COUNT GATE beside it now is.
     MissionReinforceEnabled lets a mission-held axis absorb reinforcements, so the four
     riflemen join the push and advance instead of reading adv@never. Expect the count
     gate to be SATISFIED (owed=0) and the interval to be RED at several hundred ticks -
     which is the honest reading of a number that previously printed 0/300 because only
     two units ever advanced at all. A green d1 needs a lead-hold, below.

  d2 SPREAD AT THE MIDLINE - NOT addressed, and it is now understood well enough to say
     that no ordering or claim fix can ever address it. It is a SPEED clause, not a
     departure clause. An abrams is Speed 90 (ingame/vehicles-america.yaml:521) and a
     rifleman is Speed 25, inherited unmodified down E3.america -> ^E3 -> ^CamoSoldier ->
     ^Soldier -> ^Infantry (ingame/infantry.yaml:47-48; no Speed or SpeedMultiplier
     override anywhere on that chain). Both start at x~4 and are ordered to the same objective
     with no lead-hold anywhere in the bot modules, so at the instant the tank has covered
     the 29 cells to x=33 the rifleman has covered 29 x 25/90 ~ 8, and the bounding box is
     ~21 cells wide. That is arithmetic, not tuning: d2 <= 8 is unreachable while the fast
     element does not wait. Measured 18 before this batch and expected to stay in the same
     band after it, because the riflemen were already walking to a muster at x~15-18 and
     the fix changes where they are GOING, not how fast they get there.

     THE 2026-09-05 "MARCH BACK TO THE MUSTER" READING OF THIS CLAUSE IS RETRACTED. This
     file used to assert that a mission-committed axis is excluded from the axis set before
     BuildFreePool "so its own units read as free pool and StageFreePool staged them back".
     The code forbids it: PartitionHeldAxes refreshes goalGuard.Ledger.Commit for every
     held-axis unit (PoiOffensiveBotModule.cs, "HELD: keep the mission") BEFORE BuildFreePool
     runs, and BuildFreePool excludes every ledger-committed actor. A held axis's units
     cannot be in the free pool. What IS true, and is what stranded the riflemen, is the
     other half of the same exclusion: a held axis is invisible to the allocator, so it is
     never TOPPED UP - and because held targets are stripped from the candidate list, with
     one offensive POI in the world DesiredAxisCount gets poiCount=0 and no axis can form
     for anybody either. That is what MissionReinforceEnabled fixes. The new [exp-ledger]
     census line in debug.log names the module holding every excluded unit, so this
     particular ambiguity cannot recur unmeasured.

So this scenario is a measuring instrument first and a gate second. Its NOTE is the
product: three numbers plus a per-unit "born@N left@M adv@K at=x,y" roll, in
result.json, every run.

DELETE THIS FILE when a lead-hold (the fast element waits at a bound for the slow one)
lands and d1/d2 can be met. Until then the declared outcome occurring prints OK(fail);
the declared outcome ceasing to occur prints STALE and reds, which is the whole point of
declaring it.

A crash, a hang, or a run that never reaches a verdict still reds under this
declaration - it only ever buys silence for the one outcome it names.
